Let $I=\{1\}$, $A=(2)$, so that $U_q(\mathfrak g)=U_q(\mathfrak{sl}_2)$ with generators $E,F,K^{\pm1}$, $q_1=q$, and let $m\in\mathbb Z$. The quantum Verma module $M(m)=M(\lambda)$ with $\lambda(h_1)=m$ has $\mathbb Q(q)$-basis $\{v_t:=F^tv_0\}_{t\ge0}$ with $v_0=v_\lambda$, weights $m-2t$, and actions
$$Kv_t=q^{m-2t}v_t,\qquad Fv_t=v_{t+1},\qquad Ev_t=[m-t+1]_qv_{t-1}\quad(t\ge1),\qquad Ev_0=0,$$
as follows from [[thm-quantized-sl-two-string-formulas]](iii) with $N=m$ when $m\ge0$ and by the same computation in general. In particular:

(a) if $m\ge0$, the submodule generated by $F^{m+1}v_0$ is $\bigoplus_{t\ge m+1}\mathbb Q(q)v_t$, the quotient $\tilde L(m)=M(m)/U_qF^{m+1}v_0$ is the $(m+1)$-dimensional simple module with basis $v_0,\dots,v_m$, $Kv_t=q^{m-2t}v_t$, $Fv_t=[t+1]_qv_{t+1}$, $Ev_t=[m-t+1]_qv_{t-1}$, and for $m=0$ this is the one-dimensional trivial module;

(b) if $m=-1$, then $[m-t+1]_q=[-t]_q=-[t]_q\ne0$ for all $t\ge1$, so no nonzero vector of positive weight-string index is killed by $E$ and $M(-1)$ is irreducible of infinite dimension: the simple module $L(-1)$ is not integrable, and the highest weight is not dominant integral;

(c) for every $m<0$ the string coefficient $[m-t+1]_q$ vanishes only at the nonpositive integer $t=m+1\le0$, so as in (b) the Verma module $M(m)$ is irreducible, infinite-dimensional and not integrable; the dominance hypothesis $m\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}$ in [[thm-integrable-quantum-highest-weight-module-exists-and-is-unique]] cannot be relaxed.
